## Chapter 1: What is the Physical Layer?

The OSI model has seven layers. Layer 1 — the Physical Layer — is the absolute foundation. It is the actual physical medium that carries data: the copper wire, the fibre optic cable, the Wi-Fi radio wave, the electrical signal on a network port. Every single packet that travels anywhere must first exist as a physical signal at Layer 1.

Most people dismiss Layer 1 as "just cables and hardware." This is one of the most dangerous misconceptions in cybersecurity. From an attacker's perspective, Layer 1 is incredibly valuable — because if you can reach the physical layer, you bypass every software control sitting above it.

> ⚠️ **The Golden Rule of Physical Security**
>
> "Physical access is total access."
>
> If an attacker can physically reach a device, they can:
> - Boot from a USB drive and bypass Windows authentication entirely
> - Remove the hard drive and read it on their own machine
> - Install a hardware keylogger and record every password typed
> - Tap the network cable and passively capture all traffic
>
> No firewall, no SIEM, no endpoint agent stops any of these — because they all happen before or outside the operating system's awareness.

### Why Layer 1 Matters in a SOC

Layer 1 attacks are unique in a critical way: **no software tool can detect them directly.** Wireshark cannot see a hardware tap. Sysmon cannot detect a keylogger between a keyboard and a PC. Zeek cannot alert on someone walking into a server room.

This means Layer 1 security depends entirely on a different set of controls: physical barriers, policies, access logs, CCTV, and mobile device management.

> 💡 **SOC Analyst Perspective**
>
> You will rarely "detect" a Layer 1 attack from network logs alone. What you will do is **correlate physical events with digital anomalies:**
> - After-hours badge swipe to server room → followed by unusual network traffic
> - New unregistered device MAC appearing on the network
> - USB device event in Sysmon → followed by malware execution
> - Employee reports missing laptop → MDM triggered remote wipe

