## Chapter 2: Layer 1 Threat Landscape

### Threat 1 — Unauthorised Physical Access

The most fundamental Layer 1 threat. If an attacker gains physical access to a device or infrastructure, the consequences cascade through every layer above.

| What the attacker can do | Why software cannot stop it | SOC / IT response |
|---|---|---|
| Boot from USB and bypass Windows login | BIOS/UEFI runs before the OS — no endpoint agent is active | BIOS password + boot order lock + full disk encryption |
| Remove hard drive and read it elsewhere | The OS is not running — no encryption means full data exposure | BitLocker/FileVault — without the key, the drive is unreadable |
| Install a hardware keylogger | Physical device between keyboard and PC — no process, no driver, nothing for Sysmon | Physical port blocking, locked PC cases, regular hardware audits |
| Connect a network tap to a cable | A passive device copies traffic silently — no packets generated | Cable management, locked comms rooms, Zeek detecting new MACs |
| Access server room infrastructure | RAID arrays, switches, routers — all physically accessible | Mantrap, biometric access, CCTV, visitor escort policy |

> 🎓 **CompTIA Security+ SY0-701 — Domain 2.0 Physical Threats**
>
> Physical attacks are explicitly tested. Know these scenarios:
> - **Tailgating / piggybacking** — following someone through a secure door without badging
> - **Shoulder surfing** — watching someone type credentials in a public space
> - **Dumpster diving** — recovering sensitive documents or hardware from rubbish
> - **Theft of devices** — laptops, phones, USB drives containing corporate data
>
> Physical security controls tested in Domain 3.0 Security Architecture:
> - Bollards, mantraps, badge readers, CCTV, security guards, cable locks
> - Faraday cages (block RF signals — used in high-security environments)
> - Secure destruction of decommissioned hardware (degaussing, shredding)

