### Threat 2 — USB Drop Attacks

An attacker leaves a USB drive in a location where a target employee is likely to find it: a car park, reception desk, bathroom, or conference room. Human curiosity does the rest.

| USB attack type | What it does | Why it is effective | Detection |
|---|---|---|---|
| Malware payload | Auto-executes malware when inserted | Employees are curious — they want to know what is on the drive | Sysmon EID 6416 (new device) + EID 1 (process execution from USB path) |
| BadUSB / Rubber Ducky | Identifies as a keyboard and types pre-programmed commands at machine speed | Looks like a normal USB drive — types 100 commands in 2 seconds | Sysmon EID 1 — commands executed with no user interaction |
| USB Killer | Sends high-voltage power surge — destroys the motherboard instantly | Physical destruction — no recovery possible | Cannot be detected by software — only port blocking prevents it |
| Data exfiltration drive | Employee copies sensitive files to USB | Insider threat — employee has legitimate access | DLP tools, Sysmon removable media write events |

> 💡 **SOC Detection — USB Events in Splunk**
>
> ```spl
> # Detect new USB device connections
> index=wineventlog EventCode=6416
> | table _time, host, DeviceName, DeviceId
>
> # Detect process execution from removable media
> index=sysmon EventCode=1
> | regex Image="^[D-Z]:\\"
> | table _time, host, Image, CommandLine, User
> ```
>
> Alert logic: New unsigned USB device + process execution within 60 seconds = **HIGH severity alert**

