### Threat 3 — Hardware Keyloggers

A hardware keylogger is a small device plugged between a keyboard cable and the PC. It silently records every keystroke to internal storage. The attacker returns later to retrieve the device and read the logs.

> ⚠️ **Why This is Extremely Hard to Detect**
>
> Hardware keyloggers are completely transparent to the operating system.
> No new process. No new network connection. No driver installation.
> Sysmon sees nothing. Antivirus sees nothing. EDR sees nothing.
>
> **Detection methods:**
> - Physical inspection — visually check all keyboard connections regularly
> - USB port auditing — photograph all port connections as baseline
> - Locked PC cases — prevent access to rear USB ports
> - Wireless keyboards — some keyloggers work on RF too (KeySweeper)

