### Threat 4 — Network Taps and Rogue Devices

A passive network tap splices into a cable and copies all traffic to a capture device. Unlike an active device, a passive tap generates no traffic of its own and is essentially invisible to network monitoring.

| Device type | How it works | What it captures | Detection method |
|---|---|---|---|
| Passive network tap | Physical splice into cable — copies all bits silently | Everything: HTTP, TLS metadata, DNS, all protocols | Cannot be detected remotely — requires physical cable inspection |
| Rogue switch | Plugged into open port — creates new network segment | All traffic on connected devices | New MAC/IP in Zeek conn.log; DHCP anomaly |
| Rogue Wi-Fi AP | Creates evil twin or open hotspot | Credentials, session cookies, unencrypted traffic | Wireless IDS; Zeek 802.11 logs; duplicate SSID detection |
| USB implant (LAN Turtle) | Plugs into USB port — creates reverse tunnel to attacker | All traffic on the host machine | Sysmon new network connection; unusual outbound in OPNsense |

> 💡 **Lab Connection**
>
> In your lab, Zeek on Ubuntu ELK (10.10.10.30) monitors `conn.log` for all connections.
> A rogue device on VMnet10 would appear as a new, unrecognised MAC address.
>
> ```spl
> index=zeek sourcetype=zeek_conn
> | stats earliest(_time) as first_seen by id.orig_h
> | where first_seen > relative_time(now(), "-1d@d")
> ```
>
> Any IP not in your known asset list (10.10.10.2, .5, .10, .20, .30) = investigate immediately.

