## Chapter 3: Mobile Device Management (MDM)

MDM is software that allows IT and security teams to remotely manage, monitor, and control enrolled mobile devices. For a SOC analyst, MDM is both a **detection source** and a **response tool**.

### Full MDM Capability Map

| MDM capability | Plain English explanation | SOC / Security relevance |
|---|---|---|
| Remote wipe | Erase all data on the device — factory reset remotely | Stolen device: trigger wipe before attacker accesses data |
| Remote lock | Lock device with passcode the user cannot bypass | Suspected compromise: lock immediately during investigation |
| Force disk encryption | Require full device encryption (BitLocker/FileVault/native) | Stolen encrypted device is unreadable without the key |
| Application allowlisting | Only pre-approved apps can install or run | Blocks malware, unapproved tools, data exfiltration apps |
| Jailbreak / root detection | Alert when device security model is bypassed | Jailbroken device = MDM controls may be circumvented |
| Geolocation tracking | Show current GPS location of enrolled device | Verify "I left it at home" claims; locate stolen devices |
| Certificate deployment | Push corporate TLS certificates to device trust store | Enables TLS inspection proxy to work on mobile |
| Wi-Fi profile enforcement | Auto-connect to corporate Wi-Fi with correct security | Prevents connection to rogue access points |
| Compliance enforcement | Block non-compliant devices from corporate resources | No MDM + no encryption = no network access |
| OS update enforcement | Require minimum OS version; force updates | Ensures devices are patched against known vulnerabilities |
| Screen lock policy | Enforce auto-lock after inactivity | Prevents physical access when device is unattended |

> 💡 **MDM Events in SIEM — What SOC Analysts Look For**
>
> High-priority MDM alerts for SOC triage:
> - **Remote wipe triggered** — correlate with who reported device lost/stolen and when
> - **Jailbreak detected** — treat as potentially compromised; revoke access immediately
> - **Compliance failure** — device accessing network without MDM or encryption
> - **New device enrolled** — verify this is a known employee and approved device
> - **Geolocation anomaly** — device in a different country than the employee's badge swipes

### BYOD vs Corporate Devices

| Aspect | Corporate-managed device | BYOD (personal device) | SOC implication |
|---|---|---|---|
| MDM control | Full MDM — complete remote management | Containerised MDM — only corporate data managed | BYOD limits SOC response — cannot wipe whole device |
| Disk encryption | Enforced — no exceptions | Encouraged but often not enforceable | Stolen BYOD + no encryption = potential NDB notification |
| App control | Strict allowlisting | Limited — personal apps cannot be controlled | Personal apps may exfiltrate data from work container |
| Incident response | Full forensic access | Limited — personal data protected | SOC scope limited on personal devices |
| Remote wipe | Full wipe authorised | Only corporate container wiped | Faster containment on corporate devices |

