## Chapter 5: MITRE ATT&CK — Layer 1 Techniques

| Technique ID | Tactic | Name | How it works at Layer 1 | Detection in your lab |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| T1091 | Initial Access / Lateral Movement | Replication Through Removable Media | Malware auto-executes when USB drive is inserted | Sysmon EID 6416 + EID 1 (process from USB path) |
| T1200 | Initial Access | Hardware Additions | Rogue switch, AP, keylogger, or LAN Turtle plugged into network port | New MAC in Zeek conn.log; Sysmon new device events |
| T1052.001 | Exfiltration | Exfiltration Over Physical Medium — USB | Insider copies data to USB drive for removal | Sysmon removable media write; DLP alerts; EID 4663 |
| T1556.006 | Credential Access | MFA — Hardware Token Theft | Physical theft of hardware MFA token (RSA SecurID, YubiKey) | User reports lost token; correlate with unusual logins |
| T1498 | Impact | Network Denial of Service (physical) | Cable cutting, power disruption, hardware destruction | Loss of connectivity; availability monitoring alerts |
| T1592.001 | Reconnaissance | Gather Victim Host Info — Hardware | Physically inspect visible hardware labels, asset tags | Visitor log anomalies; CCTV; no digital detection |
| T1219 | Command & Control | Remote Access via Implant | LAN Turtle creates persistent reverse tunnel via USB | Sysmon outbound connection; OPNsense firewall alert |

> 📌 **Memory Tip for ATT&CK Technique IDs**
>
> - **T1091** — Removable media (USB malware spread)
> - **T1200** — Hardware additions (rogue devices)
> - **T1052** — Physical exfiltration (USB data theft)
>
> Always include the technique ID in incident reports — it maps your finding to a universal framework any SOC team can understand immediately.

