## Chapter 8: Knowledge Check

| Question | Answer |
|---|---|
| What is the golden rule of physical security? | Physical access is total access — software controls can be bypassed if attacker reaches Layer 1 |
| Name three things an attacker can do with physical access to a PC | Boot from USB to bypass login; remove hard drive; install hardware keylogger; connect network tap |
| What is a BadUSB attack and why can antivirus not detect it? | USB device identifies as HID/keyboard and types commands. Antivirus scans file systems — HID devices have no file system to scan |
| What is the MITRE ATT&CK ID for removable media attacks? | T1091 — Replication Through Removable Media |
| What Windows event ID fires when a new USB device is connected? | EID 6416 — A new external device was recognised by the system |
| Name four MDM capabilities relevant to a stolen device incident | Remote wipe, remote lock, geolocation tracking, encryption enforcement |
| What is the difference between MDM and MAM? | MDM manages the whole device. MAM manages only applications — used in BYOD |
| What is juice jacking and what is the defence? | Compromised public USB charging point. Defence: AC adapters or USB data blockers |
| Which NIST publication covers mobile device security? | NIST SP 800-124r2 — Guidelines for Managing Mobile Device Security |
| Under Australian law, when does a stolen laptop become a notifiable data breach? | When device contains personal information AND was not encrypted — NDB notification within 30 days |

